\subsection{Working with a GPU table}{

A \code{tbl_gpu} works with the dplyr verbs. What the GPU can't do, dplyr does on
the CPU, and the result comes back to the GPU as a \code{tbl_gpu} (see
\link{cuplyr-fallback} to be told when that happens).
\itemize{
\item \link[=collect.tbl_gpu]{collect()} runs the pending steps and brings the
result back to R as a tibble (a plain \code{data.frame} if you uploaded
one).
\item \link[=compute.tbl_gpu]{compute()} runs the pending steps and keeps the
result on the GPU, as a \code{tbl_gpu} with nothing pending: use it for a
result you use more than once.
\item \link[=explain.tbl_gpu]{explain()} and \link[=show_query.tbl_gpu]{show_query()}
print the pending plan, one dplyr call per step, without running it.
\item \code{print()} and \code{glimpse()} show the dimensions, the column types
and the first rows.
}
}

\subsection{Lazy and eager execution}{

A \code{tbl_gpu} is lazy by default: each verb adds a step to a plan, and the
GPU does the work when a result is needed: \code{collect()}, \code{compute()},
printing, \code{nrow()}/\code{dim()}, \code{pull()} or \code{$}. A few steps need the result
of the pending plan as soon as they are called, and run it then, on the
GPU (see "Plan breaks" in \link{cuplyr-fallback}). Before a plan runs,
cuplyr optimizes it: columns no later step
reads are dropped early, consecutive \code{mutate()} calls are combined,
\code{filter()} conditions move closer to the data (below a \code{mutate()} they
don't depend on, or into an input of a join when that can't change the
join's result, errors or warnings, for example below a cross join or a
join with \code{relationship = "many-to-many"}), and of several \code{filter()}
calls in a row the cheaper conditions run first.

Errors in a verb's arguments (a missing column, a type error) are still
raised when the verb is called. Printing a lazy table with pending
operations (or calling \code{glimpse()} on it) runs the plan to show its first
rows, under a \verb{# A tbl_gpu [lazy]:} header; the table itself is unchanged and
nothing is kept, so printing it twice runs the plan twice (\code{compute()} it
first to look at it repeatedly). If the plan fails (an error, out of GPU
memory), the print shows the schema and the message instead of raising.

In eager mode (\code{tbl_gpu(df, lazy = FALSE)}), each dplyr verb runs on the
GPU at once. This is simple but can lead to unnecessary intermediate
allocations. \code{options(cuplyr.exec_mode = "eager")} (or the environment
variable \code{CUPLYR_EXEC_MODE=eager}) makes every new \code{tbl_gpu} eager, and
\code{\link[=as_eager]{as_eager()}} and \code{\link[=as_lazy]{as_lazy()}} switch one table.

For measured timings and memory use of the two modes, see
\code{vignette("performance", package = "cuplyr")}.
}

\subsection{Column types}{

Each R column type is stored as a GPU type, the type the table's schema
shows:
\itemize{
\item \code{numeric} (double) -> FLOAT64
\item \code{integer} -> INT32
\item \code{bit64::integer64} -> INT64, exact. \code{collect()} gives a
\code{bit64::integer64} back (a double, with a warning past 2^53, when
bit64 is not installed).
\item \code{character} -> STRING
\item \code{logical} -> BOOL8
\item \code{factor} (ordered too) -> DICTIONARY32: the integer codes are on
the GPU and the levels stay in R, so \code{collect()} gives the factor back
with the same levels.
\item \code{Date} -> TIMESTAMP_DAYS
\item \code{POSIXct} -> TIMESTAMP_MICROSECONDS, the instant in UTC on the
GPU with the time zone kept alongside, so \code{collect()} gives the same
zone back.
}

A \code{vctrs::unspecified()} column (all \code{NA}, no type yet) is stored as BOOL8.
It collects back as \code{vctrs::unspecified()} only while it passes through
\code{filter()}, \code{arrange()}, \code{slice()} and \code{semi_join()}/\code{anti_join()} (as a
column of \code{x}, as in dplyr); every other verb (\code{select()}, \code{mutate()},
\code{group_by()} with computed expressions, \code{slice_min()}/\code{slice_max()}, a
mutating join, \code{vctrs::vec_slice()}) returns it as a plain logical column.
An all-\code{NA} logical or unspecified column joins a string, \code{Date}, \code{POSIXct}
or factor key, as in dplyr: a merged key takes that key's type; with
\code{keep = TRUE}, in a non-equi condition and in \code{semi_join()}/\code{anti_join()}
each key column keeps its own type. A logical key with a value in it
errors. Bound to a character column in \code{bind_rows()}, an unspecified
column errors.

A matrix column with more than one column (or more than two dimensions), a
list column, a data frame column and a complex or raw column have no GPU
form: \code{tbl_gpu()} raises \code{cuplyr_error_unsupported_class} naming the column.
A verb that ran on the CPU and returned a matrix or array raises
\code{cuplyr_error_unsupported_result_class} the same way; one that returned a
list or data frame column hands back a plain tibble.
}

\subsection{Dates and times the GPU can't hold}{

A GPU \code{Date} holds whole days in an int32 and a \code{POSIXct} holds int64
microseconds. A value that doesn't fit becomes \code{NA} when it is uploaded, with
a warning:
\itemize{
\item \code{cuplyr_warning_infinite_timestamp}: \code{Inf} or \code{-Inf}.
\item \code{cuplyr_warning_timestamp_out_of_range}: a finite value beyond about
5.8 million years either side of 1970 (\code{Date}) or 292,000 years
(\code{POSIXct}).
}
Both also inherit \code{cuplyr_warning_timestamp_unrepresentable}, so one handler
covers both. An upload raises at most one warning per class, listing every
affected column with its count, e.g. \verb{`d` (2 values), `t` (1 value)}.
\code{NA} and \code{NaN} are ordinary missing values and never warn. The same applies
to every other way values reach the GPU: \code{copy = TRUE} joins,
\code{bind_rows()}, \verb{$<-}, a constant in \code{mutate()}, and the result of a verb
that ran on the CPU. A fractional \code{Date} is floored with its own warning,
\code{cuplyr_warning_fractional_date}.
}
}
\examples{
library(dplyr)
if (has_gpu()) {
  # Transfer mtcars to GPU (lazy by default)
  gpu_mtcars <- tbl_gpu(mtcars)
  print(gpu_mtcars)

  # Operations are deferred until collect()
  result <- tbl_gpu(mtcars) |>
    filter(mpg > 20) |>
    mutate(kpl = mpg * 0.425) |>
    collect()

  # Eager mode: each verb runs at once
  eager_tbl <- tbl_gpu(mtcars, lazy = FALSE)

  # Or make every new table eager:
  # options(cuplyr.exec_mode = "eager")
}
}
